---
title: 'Exploring the LLM Pareto frontier of quality, cost and speed'
---

Stencila Cloud's model router provides you with the "best" model for your input.
It does this by analyzing the outputs of various benchmarking services to
produce normalized scores for quality, speed and cost.

This document describes how that's achieved.

```python exec always

import matplotlib.pyplot as plt
import numpy as np
import pandas as pd

df = pd.read_parquet("../data/routing/scoring.parquet")
all_models_df = pd.read_parquet("../data/routing/scoring-with-incomplete.parquet")

model_names = df["model"].unique()
```

::: table

How each model scores (extracted from `all_models_df`).

```python exec auto
all_models_df = pd.read_parquet("../data/routing/scoring-with-incomplete.parquet")

all_models_df[["model", "quality", "speed", "cost"]].dropna().round(2)
```

:::

::: table

How each model scores (extracted from `df`).

```python exec auto
df = pd.read_parquet("../data/routing/scoring.parquet")

df[["model", "quality", "speed", "cost"]].dropna()
```

:::

As you can see, the scales for each of the metrics are very different. To allow
them to be more easily compared, we normalize them to a 0-100 scale by using
each model's percentile ranking as the basis of comparison.

::: table

How each model scores (normalized) (extracted from `df`).

```python exec auto
df[["model", "quality_pct", "speed_pct", "cost_pct"]].dropna().round(2)
```

:::

You may notice that percentiles are not spread between 0 and 100. This is because the
percentiles are calculated based on the number of models that have a score for that metric,
but these tables only refer to models that have scores for all three metrics.

## Data sources

Stencila makes use of publicly-available, open access data sets to evaluate the
models that are available to its users:

::: table

Model scores of each metric.

| Metric  | Data source                               | Provider                         |
| ------- | ----------------------------------------- | -------------------------------- |
| Quality | Elo rating system                         | Chatbot Arena (previously LMSYS) |
| Speed   | Throughput, measured as tokens per second | Thefastest.ai                    |
| Cost    | Publicly-available pricing data           | LiteLLM                          |

:::

# Quality

Quality scores are derived from the [Chatbot Arena](https://llarena.ai) data by
LMArena and UC Berkeley Skylab. These scores are then normalized to a 0-100
scale by taking each model's percentile ranking within the range of the final
ratings.

The Chatbot Arena works by creating "battles" that allow human users provide
prompts to two models, and then assess the model's responses. People can rate
the responses as a win for one side, both equally good, or neither satisfied the 
response adequately. These last two options are considered a draw.

The results from all of battles are used to calculate an overall score for
each model. Models with the highest score are more likely to win future battles,
even against opponents that they've never met before. The formula to calculate
the score is described by what is known as an _Elo rating system_.

An _Elo rating system_ gets its name from its creator, Élő Árpád Imre, often
anglicized as Arpad Elo. It was originally designed to rank chess players, but
is now frequently used to rate sports teams and competitors in other 1v1
activities. The system works by assigning a starting numerical rating to each
player, typically 1000, and then updating that rating based on the outcome of
each game played. The amount that a player's rating changes after a game depends
on the difference in ratings between the two players.

Unlike chess and other sports though, the Chatbot Arena's scoring does not treat
recent battles as more important than older ones. In a traditional Elo rating
system, the score of a player is updated after each game. This has the effect of
giving weight to more recent results.  That's important because a player's skill
level changes over time. Large language models on the other hand are fixed once
they're released. To compensate for that, Chatbot Arena uses a derivative of the Elo
rating system called the Bradley-Terry model.

Bradley-Terry assigns a rating to each model that is most likely to correspond
to the results that have actually occurred. 

The results are somewhat susceptible to manipulation. A battle's prompts and
the evaluations of the responses are a best-effort attempt. The creators of
the Chatbot Area have done some analysis by comparing their results to other
datasets (Anthropic HH ([Bai et al., 2022]) and OpenAssistant Conversations
([Köpf et al., 2023])), and found high correspondence.

